  "response": "Here's a Python function that formats a number as currency with a dollar sign at the beginning, commas separating every three digits from the right, and a minus sign before the dollar sign for negative numbers:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    return f\"${n:,}\"\n```\n\nThis function leverages Python's built-in string formatting, which handles:\n- Positive and negative integers or floats correctly.\n- Comma separation every three digits.\n- A leading minus sign before the dollar sign for negative values.\n\n### Example Usage:\n\n```python\nprint(format_currency(4640039))        # $4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(-4640039))       # -$4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(1234.56))        # $1,234.56\nprint(format_currency(-1000000))       # -$1,000,000\nprint(format_currency(0))              # $0\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- For floats with decimals (e.g., currency values), this function formats them correctly with two decimal places if needed. If you want to enforce exactly two decimal places, use:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    return f\"${n:,.2f}\"\n```\n\nThis ensures that values like 1000 become `$1,000.00`, which is typical for currency formatting. Choose the version depending on whether you want to include decimal places.",
